1
00:00:15,212 --> 00:00:16,238
!

2
00:00:17,418 --> 00:00:19,397
Hmm...

3
00:00:19,720 --> 00:00:22,220
(Section Chief Yobe) Stop causing trouble for other people!

4
00:00:23,920 --> 00:00:24,920
That's it!

5
00:00:25,280 --> 00:00:27,040
Akane isn't "other people"!

6
00:00:27,720 --> 00:00:30,156
I can just ask Akane for help!

7
00:00:30,980 --> 00:00:33,290
Akaneeee!

8
00:00:33,520 --> 00:00:34,520
Here we go.

9
00:00:35,200 --> 00:00:37,799
Akaneeeeeee!

10
00:00:38,900 --> 00:00:42,914
It's all my fault... It's all my fault...

11
00:00:42,983 --> 00:00:44,842
Please calm down.

12
00:00:45,330 --> 00:00:46,330
It's okay.

13
00:00:46,715 --> 00:00:49,400
If you turn yourself in, the sentence will be lighter.

14
00:00:49,533 --> 00:00:52,900
I didn't do anything!

15
00:00:53,250 --> 00:00:56,050
This one's contradicting herself already.

16
00:00:58,430 --> 00:01:01,490
It seems like the transport management system is having issues too.

17
00:01:02,030 --> 00:01:03,190
All services are suspended.

18
00:01:03,990 --> 00:01:04,990
What!?

19
00:01:05,310 --> 00:01:08,121
That's outside our jurisdiction, so we don't have much info...

20
00:01:08,822 --> 00:01:10,588
Could it be the same incident?

21
00:01:10,930 --> 00:01:13,430
If so, that narrows down the possibilities.

22
00:01:14,750 --> 00:01:19,190
The transport management network and the information/business network are separated, right?

23
00:01:20,130 --> 00:01:22,383
The only things connected to both networks

24
00:01:22,497 --> 00:01:26,930
are the authentication server and the update distribution server for terminals.

25
00:01:27,140 --> 00:01:28,510
Could those be infected with malware?

26
00:01:31,230 --> 00:01:32,810
There's one more possibility.

27
00:01:33,710 --> 00:01:37,591
The possibility that an insider is deliberately attacking us.

28
00:01:39,870 --> 00:01:42,590
Right... Not something we'd want to hear, but...

29
00:01:55,740 --> 00:01:56,740
Logs?

30
00:01:57,490 --> 00:01:59,020
Retention periods vary.

31
00:01:59,975 --> 00:02:01,880
Some may already be gone.

32
00:02:02,320 --> 00:02:05,360
But there may still be important traces remaining.

33
00:02:06,260 --> 00:02:08,626
Let's gather and preserve the logs together.

34
00:02:08,893 --> 00:02:10,900
CIO Nakafunyu?

35
00:02:10,975 --> 00:02:12,793
O-Okay...

36
00:02:12,993 --> 00:02:15,460
(Hiroka) Report to the president without Director Katsuragi finding out.

37
00:02:15,593 --> 00:02:17,560
(Hiroka) Make sure it goes smoothly.

38
00:02:17,680 --> 00:02:18,680
What!?

39
00:02:18,820 --> 00:02:19,820
Me!?

40
00:02:23,060 --> 00:02:25,200
O-Okay, fine...

41
00:02:27,865 --> 00:02:30,880
Um, can I go back to my desk?

42
00:02:31,898 --> 00:02:34,632
The atmosphere here is the worst.

43
00:02:35,140 --> 00:02:36,140
Wait.

44
00:02:36,340 --> 00:02:37,380
Hosono, stay there.

45
00:02:38,050 --> 00:02:39,920
Share information with Transport Command.

46
00:02:41,460 --> 00:02:42,931
Ugh...

47
00:02:43,731 --> 00:02:46,640
Listen. A system isn't just computers.

48
00:02:47,140 --> 00:02:49,340
It includes people too.

49
00:02:49,830 --> 00:02:54,440
Properly communicating the situation to users is also a vital role for an engineer.

50
00:02:57,500 --> 00:02:58,688
Oh, hello.

51
00:02:58,822 --> 00:03:00,755
Huh? Who the hell are you?

52
00:03:01,380 --> 00:03:03,288
I'm Hosono from the PR Department, System Division.

53
00:03:03,555 --> 00:03:04,855
Hah? PR?

54
00:03:05,240 --> 00:03:06,240
Hello.

55
00:03:06,400 --> 00:03:09,588
It's the System Division, freeloading under the PR Department due to budget constraints.

56
00:03:10,288 --> 00:03:12,188
Yeah, I know. What do you want?

57
00:03:12,880 --> 00:03:14,302
Sorry for the verbal notice, but

58
00:03:14,327 --> 00:03:16,746
I have something urgent to tell everyone.

59
00:03:23,860 --> 00:03:24,950
--And that's the current situation.

60
00:03:25,655 --> 00:03:28,874
Well, to borrow Director Katsuragi's words,

61
00:03:28,986 --> 00:03:32,408
it's basically a "disciplinary action for every employee" kind of situation.

62
00:03:32,741 --> 00:03:34,474
Are you sure about this!?

63
00:03:34,670 --> 00:03:38,250
Um, you're scaring me, could you lower your tone?

64
00:03:38,690 --> 00:03:40,150
...Are you sure about this?

65
00:03:40,885 --> 00:03:42,110
We can't confirm it yet.

66
00:03:42,990 --> 00:03:46,178
However, considering what's actually happening,

67
00:03:46,202 --> 00:03:50,356
I believe we need to act on the safest possible assumption.

68
00:03:50,423 --> 00:03:52,290
Damn... This is terrible...

69
00:03:53,170 --> 00:03:55,046
A cyberattack is suspected.

70
00:03:55,113 --> 00:03:57,630
The transport management system cannot be trusted.

71
00:03:58,130 --> 00:03:59,602
Is that a fair assessment?

72
00:03:59,646 --> 00:04:00,669
...Yes.

73
00:04:00,694 --> 00:04:02,047
How long have you known?

74
00:04:02,190 --> 00:04:04,580
Since around 9 AM.

75
00:04:04,605 --> 00:04:06,713
Why didn't you say something sooner!?

76
00:04:06,950 --> 00:04:10,710
Thorough communication! Accident prevention! Compliance with regulations!

77
00:04:11,430 --> 00:04:12,530
It's on the back of your badge!

78
00:04:13,430 --> 00:04:14,430
I'm sorry.

79
00:04:16,250 --> 00:04:19,974
Wait. Whose fault was it that safety wasn't maintained?

80
00:04:20,108 --> 00:04:22,219
Why did it come to this?

81
00:04:28,390 --> 00:04:30,490
It's all my fault, isn't it?

82
00:04:31,250 --> 00:04:32,250
Hey, Yabase.

83
00:04:33,400 --> 00:04:36,205
You held back the report because you were afraid of me, right?

84
00:04:36,355 --> 00:04:40,988
Uh, well, um...

85
00:04:42,010 --> 00:04:44,038
That's part of it...

86
00:04:44,488 --> 00:04:47,722
But Director Katsuragi...

87
00:04:50,050 --> 00:04:51,070
...I'm sorry.

88
00:04:51,430 --> 00:04:52,430
It was my fault.

89
00:04:53,350 --> 00:04:56,080
Thank you for being honest.

90
00:04:56,447 --> 00:04:58,163
Everyone, listen up!

91
00:04:58,420 --> 00:04:59,910
It is now 11:05 AM.

92
00:05:00,330 --> 00:05:03,930
As of this moment, we are treating this as a critical incident.

93
00:05:05,920 --> 00:05:07,430
Accidents will happen. That's inevitable.

94
00:05:07,810 --> 00:05:08,810
That's just reality.

95
00:05:09,560 --> 00:05:11,590
Zero liability accidents. Zero incidents.

96
00:05:11,890 --> 00:05:13,190
Those are just targets.

97
00:05:13,850 --> 00:05:16,570
A zero achieved through cover-ups or word games is meaningless.

98
00:05:17,170 --> 00:05:18,833
What matters most right now

99
00:05:18,967 --> 00:05:21,400
is getting our passengers to their destinations safely.

100
00:05:22,990 --> 00:05:25,970
I'm the one who caused this situation. It's my responsibility.

101
00:05:27,380 --> 00:05:29,730
I played favorites and built walls between us.

102
00:05:30,665 --> 00:05:32,610
I consider that itself an incident.

103
00:05:34,330 --> 00:05:38,550
Realizing this only now is shameful for someone in the railway industry.

104
00:05:39,350 --> 00:05:43,690
But safety can only be achieved with everyone's cooperation.

105
00:05:44,500 --> 00:05:46,763
So please, lend me your strength.

106
00:05:47,097 --> 00:05:48,763
I'm begging you.

107
00:05:51,950 --> 00:05:54,350
At that moment, everyone remembered.

108
00:05:55,980 --> 00:05:58,130
General Rules, Accident Prevention:

109
00:05:58,705 --> 00:06:02,384
"All employees shall cooperate as one in the prevention of accidents,

110
00:06:02,568 --> 00:06:06,217
and shall ensure that no harm comes to passengers or the public,

111
00:06:06,242 --> 00:06:09,251
giving their utmost effort to this end."

112
00:06:09,420 --> 00:06:12,310
Today is the day to show what our training has prepared us for.

113
00:06:13,960 --> 00:06:17,530
Everyone, follow the emergency procedures manual.

114
00:06:17,690 --> 00:06:21,610
If there's any unexpected situation or sign of danger, contact me immediately.

115
00:06:23,865 --> 00:06:25,250
False alarms are fine.

116
00:06:25,472 --> 00:06:27,755
Anyone who prevents an accident before it happens,

117
00:06:27,840 --> 00:06:29,838
anyone who contributes to damage reduction--

118
00:06:29,863 --> 00:06:35,821
no matter what Katsuragi says, I will officially commend them under my authority.

119
00:06:36,005 --> 00:06:37,288
Understood!?

120
00:06:37,610 --> 00:06:38,610
Yes, sir!

121
00:06:39,770 --> 00:06:40,770
Hey, Tsuruga.

122
00:06:41,050 --> 00:06:43,370
I'm appointing you as incident recorder.

123
00:06:44,010 --> 00:06:45,010
Begin recording.

124
00:06:45,210 --> 00:06:49,790
Understood, beginning recording.
What about video documentation?

125
00:06:50,770 --> 00:06:52,210
There happens to be a camera over there.

126
00:06:53,110 --> 00:06:54,330
Request their cooperation.

127
00:06:55,213 --> 00:06:56,780
What!?

128
00:06:57,810 --> 00:06:59,296
Hey, Yamaguchi.

129
00:06:59,680 --> 00:07:06,138
Per the manual, make a preliminary phone report to
the Risk Management Committee and the Kinki Transport Bureau's Railway Safety Division.

130
00:07:07,230 --> 00:07:10,670
The Transport Bureau--it'll be the usual guy answering the phone.

131
00:07:10,990 --> 00:07:13,730
He asks a lot of questions, so have your answers ready.

132
00:07:14,150 --> 00:07:19,690
Roger. By the way, is this classified as a railway operations accident
or a service disruption?

133
00:07:20,410 --> 00:07:21,990
Too unprecedented to say.

134
00:07:22,990 --> 00:07:28,650
"Disrupts mainline operations for over three hours"
--wouldn't it be both?

135
00:07:29,563 --> 00:07:32,663
Then for our reporting basis,

136
00:07:32,863 --> 00:07:39,870
we'll cite the Railway Accident Reporting Rules,
Article 5, Paragraph 1, Item 5, and Paragraph 2, Item 1.

137
00:07:40,440 --> 00:07:42,130
There's also a suspected cyberattack.

138
00:07:42,510 --> 00:07:45,490
Add "deemed particularly exceptional" as well.

139
00:07:46,860 --> 00:07:48,838
Root cause analysis can wait.

140
00:07:49,005 --> 00:07:51,088
How many hours to restore?

141
00:07:51,250 --> 00:07:52,821
I don't know.

142
00:07:53,110 --> 00:07:57,288
We can't even begin restoration
without first identifying the virus.

143
00:07:58,430 --> 00:08:02,230
Worst case, we'll have to set up the entire system from scratch.

144
00:08:02,990 --> 00:08:07,113
In that case, the earliest we could restore would be tomorrow's first train.

145
00:08:07,480 --> 00:08:09,513
If it takes longer, a full week...

146
00:08:09,931 --> 00:08:11,805
Understood.

147
00:08:11,938 --> 00:08:14,055
Suspended until tomorrow morning!?

148
00:08:14,310 --> 00:08:15,871
Like hell. Don't be stupid.

149
00:08:16,055 --> 00:08:17,872
Substitute block signaling.

150
00:08:17,938 --> 00:08:20,350
Substitute block signaling!? On the entire line!?

151
00:08:21,310 --> 00:08:22,805
Seriously...

152
00:08:23,005 --> 00:08:24,472
(Managura) Dead serious.

153
00:08:24,670 --> 00:08:25,670
Five hours.

154
00:08:26,200 --> 00:08:27,750
I'll buy you five hours.

155
00:08:28,370 --> 00:08:29,370
That's the limit.

156
00:08:29,980 --> 00:08:34,463
Get at least the automatic block signaling and CTC back before the morning rush.

157
00:08:36,329 --> 00:08:40,010
Nnngh!

158
00:08:43,879 --> 00:08:45,946
Meow~

159
00:08:48,410 --> 00:08:49,905
Stationmaster Tsukita!

160
00:08:51,280 --> 00:08:52,280
Are you all right!?

161
00:08:53,190 --> 00:08:55,420
We're resuming operations using the pilot communication system.

162
00:10:20,350 --> 00:10:24,820
This is Transport Command.
Attention all trains operating within our territory.

163
00:10:25,140 --> 00:10:31,000
With the restoration of automatic block signals,
we will be switching back from substitute block to automatic block sequentially--

164
00:10:32,046 --> 00:10:36,550
The signals are fixed, so why are we stopped again?

165
00:10:36,802 --> 00:10:42,616
Only the signals between stations have been restored.
The station signals are still down.

166
00:10:46,245 --> 00:10:50,888
If only the train on the opposite platform would depart...

167
00:10:50,913 --> 00:10:54,030
But they're fumbling with manual procedures over there...

168
00:10:55,780 --> 00:10:58,440
Th-Th-This is bad, driver!

169
00:10:58,940 --> 00:11:03,480
The following train already caught up to us!

170
00:11:03,481 --> 00:11:04,481
What!?

171
00:11:10,000 --> 00:11:13,777
Transport Commaaaaand!

172
00:11:15,207 --> 00:11:16,207
A deadlock?

173
00:11:16,675 --> 00:11:18,348
Yes, I'm sorry.

174
00:11:18,703 --> 00:11:20,704
During the block system changeover,

175
00:11:20,729 --> 00:11:25,163
a misidentification of train positions led to incorrect departure orders,

176
00:11:25,304 --> 00:11:28,229
resulting in trains unable to move.

177
00:11:28,445 --> 00:11:32,780
If at least the train position display had been restored,
this wouldn't have happened...

178
00:11:33,900 --> 00:11:35,700
It's a deadlock!

179
00:11:35,800 --> 00:11:36,800
Hm?

180
00:11:37,599 --> 00:11:39,917
A deadlock!? At which station?

181
00:11:40,176 --> 00:11:42,700
No, I mean inside the system.

182
00:11:43,890 --> 00:11:46,238
According to the system developer,

183
00:11:46,372 --> 00:11:50,722
it seems a deadlock is occurring in the operations rescheduling module.

184
00:11:51,900 --> 00:11:54,880
Why would a deadlock happen inside the system?

185
00:11:55,620 --> 00:11:57,760
Deadlocks are a train thing.

186
00:11:58,860 --> 00:11:59,880
Well actually,

187
00:12:00,540 --> 00:12:02,300
they happen inside systems too.

188
00:12:03,513 --> 00:12:05,863
To prevent collisions, trains

189
00:12:05,955 --> 00:12:09,979
are controlled by signals so only one train
can be in a section at a time, right?

190
00:12:10,365 --> 00:12:11,860
Yeah, that's block signaling.

191
00:12:13,420 --> 00:12:17,000
What happens if a dispatch error puts trains going opposite directions together?

192
00:12:17,610 --> 00:12:20,260
They block each other's path and can't move.

193
00:12:20,750 --> 00:12:21,940
That's a deadlock.

194
00:12:23,380 --> 00:12:25,830
In fact, data inside a system

195
00:12:25,880 --> 00:12:31,735
is also controlled so only one person can edit it at a time,
to prevent data corruption.

196
00:12:31,760 --> 00:12:32,940
Same as block signaling.

197
00:12:34,370 --> 00:12:37,680
And if data processing requests in different orders get mixed together?

198
00:12:38,080 --> 00:12:40,340
A deadlock occurs.

199
00:12:41,430 --> 00:12:42,908
So this trouble is basically

200
00:12:42,932 --> 00:12:45,628
like a deadlock at a single station

201
00:12:45,652 --> 00:12:48,220
bringing down the entire line?

202
00:12:49,160 --> 00:12:50,830
Yes, most likely.

203
00:12:51,380 --> 00:12:56,640
With this few terminals, it normally shouldn't be possible
to take down the system.

204
00:12:57,070 --> 00:12:59,167
That's what puzzled me until now.

205
00:12:59,570 --> 00:13:02,260
But with a deadlock, even two terminals can do it.

206
00:13:03,100 --> 00:13:08,021
But the operations rescheduling module is so resource-heavy we banned its use.

207
00:13:08,188 --> 00:13:11,005
Who would be using that thing?

208
00:13:11,370 --> 00:13:15,146
Whether it's a DoS attack or just a bug--that's the question.

209
00:13:15,813 --> 00:13:17,247
Dos...koi!?

210
00:13:17,840 --> 00:13:22,260
We detected suspicious traffic resembling a DoS attack from your IP address.

211
00:13:25,120 --> 00:13:27,413
DoS attack.

212
00:13:27,730 --> 00:13:29,963
DoS attack.

213
00:13:30,346 --> 00:13:31,963
Dos...koi?

214
00:13:32,113 --> 00:13:35,013
(Sumo wrestler) DOSUKOI!

215
00:15:15,207 --> 00:15:18,273
Regarding this incident, security experts say--

216
00:15:18,364 --> 00:15:21,138
Leave it to me, Akane!

217
00:15:21,207 --> 00:15:24,740
Yes, it's the team that handles security incidents.

218
00:15:24,898 --> 00:15:26,273
What did you say!?

219
00:15:26,298 --> 00:15:28,940
Do you have any idea how much damage this caused!?

220
00:15:28,965 --> 00:15:30,140
Grr!
